WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:09.368
This is the Korea Daily Brief from the CSIS Korea Chair, read by an AI voice. It's Friday, October 30.

2
00:00:09.368 --> 00:00:21.254
Three things today: Kim Jong Un fires missiles and shows off drones; South Korea's spy chief confirms Washington cut intelligence sharing; and a key nuclear deal hits a wall.

3
00:00:21.254 --> 00:00:32.799
North Korea launched around 10 ballistic missiles the same day Kim appeared at a drone weapons show, and Seoul and Washington can't agree on how many missiles flew. U.S.

4
00:00:32.799 --> 00:00:40.860
midterm elections are four days away, on November 3, and the G20 Global Financial Stability Conference opens tomorrow.

5
00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:50.259
First, Kim Jong Un spent Thursday at a drone and weapons exhibition, and North Korea fired a salvo of ballistic missiles the same day.

6
00:00:50.259 --> 00:01:01.701
Kim attended the Unmanned Aeronautical Technology Development-2026 expo, where North Korea publicly demonstrated the 600mm multiple rocket launcher it calls the Sniper for the first time.

7
00:01:01.701 --> 00:01:07.208
Live-fire demonstrations also included suicide attack drones and tactical cruise missiles.

8
00:01:07.208 --> 00:01:18.712
Speaking at the event, Kim said: "The aim to seize the absolute superiority in the drone technology is an indispensable option in the light of the ever-changing patterns of modern warfare.

9
00:01:18.712 --> 00:01:31.928
Strength precisely means peace and peace is guaranteed only by strength." Separately, Seoul's Joint Chiefs of Staff detected around 10 short-range ballistic missiles launched from the Wonsan area toward the East Sea.

10
00:01:31.928 --> 00:01:44.472
The U.S. assessment, however, counted only 2 ballistic missile launches. That discrepancy is unresolved. South Korea's National Security Office said the launches violated U.N. Security Council resolutions.

11
00:01:44.472 --> 00:01:51.447
Trilateral director-general consultations among Seoul, Washington, and Tokyo were held by phone today, October 30.

12
00:01:51.447 --> 00:02:00.992
The National Bureau of Asian Research has a new paper surveying elite opinion in Washington, Tokyo, and Seoul on the durability of the trilateral framework.

13
00:02:00.992 --> 00:02:13.108
It finds divergent threat perceptions and domestic pressures in each capital as the primary strains on Camp David commitments, and it frames those findings directly against this week's provocations.

14
00:02:13.108 --> 00:02:23.020
The first-public Sniper rocket launcher, around 10 missiles from Wonsan, and a contested count between allies: that's the picture from the Korean Peninsula today.

15
00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:34.479
Second, South Korea's intelligence chief has confirmed what had been reported but not officially acknowledged, the United States restricted intelligence sharing with Seoul.

16
00:02:34.479 --> 00:02:52.612
National Intelligence Service Director Lee Jong-seok told the National Assembly Intelligence Committee on October 29 that Washington began limiting what it shared after Unification Minister Chung Dong-young publicly disclosed information about a suspected North Korean nuclear facility earlier this year.

17
00:02:52.612 --> 00:03:04.004
Lee also told the committee that U.S. visa delays affecting National Intelligence Service personnel assigned to the U.S. Embassy have been resolved, and that those staff are working normally.

18
00:03:04.004 --> 00:03:16.888
The service characterized the delays as a tightening of global U.S. entry policy, not a bilateral dispute. The intelligence-sharing restriction adds a new dimension to an already strained moment in the alliance. U.S.

19
00:03:16.888 --> 00:03:24.284
and South Korean defense chiefs agreed on October 28 to delay wartime operational control transfer talks to early next year.

20
00:03:24.284 --> 00:03:39.136
Presidential National Security Adviser Wi Sung-lac said today that the intelligence restrictions need to be resolved and that he expects substantive progress on wartime operational control at the Security Consultative Meeting scheduled for December.

21
00:03:39.136 --> 00:03:50.529
Bloomberg this week examined constraints on Seoul's defense buildup, arguing the operational control delay and alliance tensions amount to a reality check on South Korea's military ambitions.

22
00:03:50.529 --> 00:04:00.490
The bottom line: a confirmed intelligence cutoff, a delayed operational control timeline, and a December meeting that Seoul is now treating as the next pressure point.

23
00:04:01.690 --> 00:04:05.317
Third, a major South Korean nuclear project is in trouble.

24
00:04:05.317 --> 00:04:18.575
Korea Hydro and Nuclear Power terminated negotiations with Daewoo Engineering and Construction and Doosan Enerbility over their subcontracting role in the 18.7 billion dollar Czech Dukovany nuclear plant project.

25
00:04:18.575 --> 00:04:28.957
The talks collapsed over cost disputes. Korea Hydro and Nuclear Power says the overall project timeline will not be delayed but has not named replacement contractors.

26
00:04:28.957 --> 00:04:40.089
The breakdown comes as Japan's IHI is actively competing for overseas nuclear demand in the same European market, putting pressure on Seoul to resolve the contractor gap quickly.

27
00:04:40.089 --> 00:04:44.404
The Korean government had separate nuclear energy business this week.

28
00:04:44.404 --> 00:04:57.849
South Korea hosted the International Atomic Energy Agency's International Ministerial Conference on Nuclear Power in Incheon, which closed with small modular reactors and nuclear-AI integration on the agenda. A U.S.

29
00:04:57.849 --> 00:05:05.667
State Department official, Deputy Assistant Secretary Christopher Klein, said on the margins of that conference that the U.S.

30
00:05:05.667 --> 00:05:19.050
and South Korea can achieve "great things" in small modular reactors, calling the moment a "nuclear renaissance." The 18.7 billion dollar Czech contract, no replacement contractors named: that's where things stand.

31
00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:24.894
Now to Pyongyang. State media led today with Kim's drone-supremacy call.

32
00:05:24.894 --> 00:05:37.277
KCNA covered the Unmanned Aeronautical Technology Development-2026 exhibition, including the first public firing of the 600mm Sniper launcher, that's the same story that opened this broadcast.

33
00:05:37.277 --> 00:05:45.725
State media framed it as a demonstration of Kim's vision for AI-enabled warfare. KCNA also ran an article slamming South Korea-U.S.

34
00:05:45.725 --> 00:05:53.400
Freedom Flag air defense drills, vowing that Pyongyang will counter what it calls hostile military activity with force.

35
00:05:53.400 --> 00:06:08.750
One other item from Pyongyang today: Swiss Foreign Minister Ignazio Cassis attended the opening of Switzerland's first diplomatic office in Pyongyang, the most significant Western re-engagement signal in the North Korean capital in years.

36
00:06:09.950 --> 00:06:22.981
The rest of the news, quickly. Prosecutors have asked for the death penalty for former President Yoon in his ongoing criminal trial over the martial law declaration. The sentencing request has been filed.

37
00:06:22.981 --> 00:06:36.012
On the alliance, U.S. Forces Korea Commander General Xavier Brunson called the Multi-Domain Task Force "absolutely attractive" for Korea, a pitch that has raised concerns about China contingency planning.

38
00:06:36.012 --> 00:06:42.145
General Brunson also walked the Joint Security Area with German Chancellor Friedrich Merz today.

39
00:06:42.145 --> 00:06:51.854
Defense Minister Kang Shin-chul attended the 13th ADMM-Plus in the Philippines on October 29, his first multilateral security forum since taking office.

40
00:06:51.854 --> 00:06:56.709
He held separate bilateral talks with Singapore and Vietnam on the same day.

41
00:06:56.709 --> 00:07:08.079
On South Korea's reported diplomatic tilt toward Russia: multiple Ukrainian outlets cited a Financial Times report on Seoul moving closer to Moscow as ties with Kyiv deteriorate.

42
00:07:08.079 --> 00:07:18.619
One Ukrainian outlet reported Seoul had not ruled out severing diplomatic ties with Kyiv, though the South Korean government has not confirmed that characterization.

43
00:07:18.619 --> 00:07:26.348
Republican Congressman Joe Wilson separately condemned what he called Seoul's dangerous and insulting tilt toward Russia.

44
00:07:26.348 --> 00:07:37.974
On the economy: South Korea's industrial output in September rose 3.0 percent, the largest gain in 15 and a half years, with consumer spending and investment rising at the same time.

45
00:07:37.974 --> 00:07:42.446
Seoul also unveiled its first retirement pension overhaul in 21 years.

46
00:07:42.446 --> 00:07:56.755
The plan would shift to a fund-based system with contributions pooled from multiple companies under professional management, requiring all companies to deposit workers' retirement benefits with external institutions by 2030.

47
00:07:56.755 --> 00:08:10.233
Gallup Korea's latest poll, taken October 30, finds the Democratic Party leads the People Power Party by 5 percentage points, 35 percent to 30 percent, the narrowest gap since the current government took office.

48
00:08:10.233 --> 00:08:26.203
On the special counsel front: the team investigating ballot shortages during the June 3 election in Seoul's Songpa Ward asked the National Election Commission to preserve 420 uninspected ballot boxes and transferred 13 boxes for forensic examination.

49
00:08:26.203 --> 00:08:38.020
A blockchain intelligence firm has linked North Korea to a 6.3 billion dollar hack of Binance, described as the largest cryptocurrency theft on record, surpassing the 2025 Bybit breach.

50
00:08:39.220 --> 00:08:49.806
On Capitol Hill. U.S. lawmakers are advancing legislation that would ease the transfer of American-origin munitions to Ukraine through South Korea and allied nations.

51
00:08:49.806 --> 00:09:00.328
Separately, Republican Congressman Joe Wilson publicly condemned Seoul's reported tilt toward Russia, calling it dangerous and insulting to alliance principles. U.S.

52
00:09:00.328 --> 00:09:08.810
midterm elections are November 3, which will shape the fiscal year 2027 defense appropriations outlook and Korea-related legislation.

53
00:09:10.010 --> 00:09:23.422
Beyond the region. German Chancellor Friedrich Merz said North Korea's weapons cooperation with Russia constitutes a threat to European freedom, framing the Lee-Merz strategic partnership partly around that axis.

54
00:09:23.422 --> 00:09:28.926
South Korea is weighing deployment options to the Strait of Hormuz, including aircraft.

55
00:09:28.926 --> 00:09:38.795
President Lee Jae-myung and Saudi Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman agreed by phone on October 29 to strengthen ties as "future-oriented strategic partners."

56
00:09:38.795 --> 00:09:51.890
South Korea hosted the International Atomic Energy Agency ministerial conference on nuclear power in Incheon, which closed with small modular reactors and nuclear-AI integration at the center of discussions.

57
00:09:53.090 --> 00:10:10.287
Finally, the markets. Seoul's main stock index, the KOSPI, closed at about 5,731, down roughly 3 and a half percent. The dollar bought about 1,335 won. Brent crude fell about 1 percent to just under 114 dollars a barrel.

58
00:10:10.287 --> 00:10:23.810
The Bank of Korea's policy rate stands at 3.00 percent. That's the Korea Daily Brief for Friday, October 30. The full text, with a link to every source, is in today's email.
